Ticket #4471 holds 412 reports about dates. It was merged as one issue by subject line
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), but it covered three different
problems. Each got its own ticket, and all three are closed.

## The three problems

| problem | reports | tenants | what happens |
|---|---|---|---|
| parser | 374 | 11 | The shared parser reads every date month first. Day-first tenants (for example `da-DK`, `nl-NL`, `en-GB`) get a flipped month: a whole different date, not a day's difference. |
| hint | 33 | 3 | Their booking form (booking-v3) shows `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. People type what the hint says, and the form reads it in the tenant's own day-first order. |
| not the parser bug | 5 | 5 | A date off by exactly one day, only in one month, or different by the reader's timezone. None of these move the way a month/day swap moves a date. |

The parser and hint problems are both month/day confusion, told apart by which part of the
system misreads the date. The third group was never that: it was swept into #4471 because its
subject lines matched, not because its cause did.

## What was really in the "not the parser bug" group

- kettle.books: invite said Tuesday 12 May, calendar said Monday 11 May — one day, same month.
- alder.physio: every June booking is one day off, only in June.
- pale.harbour: the same meeting showed Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto.
- lantern.row: Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday.
- nine.lanterns: the invite arrived before the booking was made.

A month/day swap turns 12 May into 5 December — a different date, not a day's difference.
Four of these five move by exactly one day or depend on timezone, which points to a DST or
timezone boundary bug, not the parser. The fifth has no date symptom at all and needs its own
look.

## What happened

- The parser cause stayed on #4471. Its own ticket here tracks it; closed.
- The hint cause got its own ticket, separate from #4471. It affects booking-v3 forms in
  `de-DE`, `pt-PT` and `es-ES`; closed.
- The 5 misfiled reports got their own ticket, pulled out of the parser count; closed.

## Notes

- Intake tags are a guess from the reporter's browser. In the parser group, 23 reports were
  tagged `hint`; in the hint group, 15 were tagged `parser`. Do not sort by intake tag.
- The dedupe rule merges on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  That is fast, but it is why a timezone bug and a parser bug can end up under the same ticket:
  nobody reads the body until someone has to split it back out.

